			<div class="container left">
				<div class="content">
					<a href="/StrikingSixties/events/fordmachinists"><b>June-July 1968</b> Ford sewing machinists' strike</a>
					<div class="timelinehover">
						Over 300 workers who produced car seats for the giant Ford Motor Company - all of them <a href="/StrikingSixties/themes/gender-and-race">women</a> - went on strike in Dagenham, Essex and Halewood, Merseyside. They took the decision to do so themselves, without union approval, after finding that the company had classed them as Category B - unskilled - in the new <a href="/StrikingSixties/themes/unions">union-approved</a> pay grading system. Over three weeks, negotations would be escalated all the way up to the height of <a>government</a>.<p>The strike has since been mythologised as a 'strike for equal pay' that heralded in the 1970 Equal Pay Act. However, historian Sheila Cohen argues that the strikers saw their strike as within the same pay-grading struggles as their male colleagues, and that 'equal pay' was imposed from outside as a means to neuter the strike. Indeed, thanks to clever manoeuvring from the government, the strikers never got their all-important up-grade. Was Ford really a 'strike for equal pay', or something more complicated?</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="container right">
				<div class="content">
					<a href="/StrikingSixties/sources/in-place-of-strife"><b>1968</b> Publication of <i>In Place of Strife</i></a>
					<div class="timelinehover">
						Written by Employment Minister Barbara Castle, this white paper proposed restrictions on the right to strike in response to a massive increase in <a href="/StrikingSixties/themes/unions">'unofficial' strikes</a>. It sparked <a href="/StrikingSixties/events/mayday1969">massive protests</a> and was shelved, but many of its proposals would be implemented in the following years.<p>As a source, it can tell us a huge amount. It includes the <a href="/StrikingSixties/themes/government">government's</a> statistics of the total hours lost to official and unofficial strikes; it exemplifies the Labour government's anti-worker response to rising unrest; and the resistance its proposals triggered can be seen as the climax of the militancy that filled the decade.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
